INT. BALLROOM OF THE GRAND CAPRICE HOTEL - NIGHT

A faded ballroom in a faded resort. A crystal chandelier swings gently, missing every third bulb. Red velvet curtains droop. A banner reads "SENIOR SINGLES MIXER" in glitter. Thirty folding chairs stand in rows, nearly empty.

A tall, stately man in a white dinner jacket stands at the doorway, shaking out a wet umbrella. He is MR. AUGUSTUS PEMBERTON-HYDE, 70s. He sweeps one white glove across his hair, adjusts his tie, and surveys the room as if reviewing troops.

At the punch bowl, a small woman in a feathered hat and an enormous corsage ladles with great seriousness. She is MRS. ODETTE FUSSELL, 60s. She hums.

Across the dance floor, a stout man in a paisley vest sits alone, tapping his foot to music only he hears. He is MR. BARTHOLOMEW "BARNEY" KRUPP, 60s.

And at the piano, in a sequined gown that hasn't fit since 1985, sits MISS FLORENCE LOVELACE, 70s, who thumps a single chord and waits.

The tall man clears his throat.

TALL MAN
Good evening.

He bows. Odette curtsies with the ladle. Barney waves. Florence thumps the chord again.

ODETTE
Mr. Pemberton-Hyde! Punch?

PEMBERTON-HYDE
Madam, I would be honored.

He crosses the floor with his stiff, ceremonious stride. She hands him a cup. He sips. His face goes through three expressions.

PEMBERTON-HYDE (CONT'D)
Is this... gravy?

ODETTE
Oh, dear.

BARNEY
(calling)
I told her it was gravy!

FLORENCE
(from the piano)
Is anyone going to dance, or must I play to the walls?

Odette takes the cup back. She and Pemberton-Hyde look at each other, and then, without a word, she takes his arm and he leads her out to the center of the floor. Florence strikes up a waltz, off-key. Barney leaps to his feet and applauds.

The chandelier swings. A bulb pops. Nobody minds.
